  "summary": "Meaning made in the cut: stone lions leaping, battleship guns, Kuleshov's hungry face.",
  "description": "Soviet montage — Kuleshov, Eisenstein, Vertov, Pudovkin — built a film theory on the cut itself: the Kuleshov effect showed identical faces acquire meaning from adjacent shots; Eisenstein's \"collision\" montage (Battleship Potemkin's Odessa Steps, October's stone lions) created ideas no single frame contained; Vertov's Man with a Movie Camera made the camera and edit themselves the hero. Visually the school favored dynamic diagonals, rhythmic mass choreography, constructivist title cards and agit-prop poster acting. Its editing grammar infiltrated advertising, music video and every action climax; its theory remains the backbone of film-school editing pedagogy.",
  "culturalContext": "Soviet montage is editing's foundational theory: the Kuleshov effect and Eisenstein's collisions taught the world that meaning lives between shots; film schools still teach the Odessa Steps, and advertising, music video and every action climax inherit the grammar.",
